remarks.para:
The gate is private to this type, so all synchronization is encapsulated here and never shared with callers.
On .NET 9+ the Lock alias resolves to System.Threading.Lock (fast-path locking);
on earlier targets it resolves to  and the lock statement falls back to .

example.code:
var resolver = new GlobalGenericFirstDependencyResolver(r =>
{
    r.Register<IService>(() => new ServiceA());
    r.Register<ILogger>(() => new ConsoleLogger());
    r.RegisterConstant(configuration);
});

member.remarks:
Registering a constant ensures that the same instance is returned for all requests matching
            the specified type and contract. If a contract is provided, the constant is associated only with that contract;
            otherwise, it is registered for the type without a contract.

remarks.para:
This resolver favors the generic API surface (, )
for performance, while still supporting non-generic APIs (, )
through an internal type registry.

example.code:
var resolver = new InstanceGenericFirstDependencyResolver(r =>
{
    r.Register<IService>(() => new ServiceA());
    r.Register<ILogger>(() => new ConsoleLogger());
    r.RegisterConstant(configuration);
});

member.remarks:
This is a copy-on-write snapshot to allow lock-free reads:
writers publish a new  instance via assignment under ;
readers use Volatile Read without locking.
